    "meaning": "to have; there is / there isn't",
    "level": "A1",
    "function": "being-and-having",
    "explanation": "有 means both 'to have' and 'there is'. Its negative is the single word 冇 — 唔有 is always wrong.",
    "writtenEquivalent": { "cjk": "有 / 沒有", "note": "沒有 is written Chinese; in speech say 冇." },
    "examples": [
      { "words": [["我", "ngo5"], ["有", "jau5"], ["一", "jat1"], ["隻", "zek3"], ["貓", "maau1"], ["。"]], "en": "I have a cat.", "gap": { "word": "有", "distractors": ["冇", "係", "喺"] } },
      { "words": [["佢", "keoi5"], ["冇", "mou5"], ["車", "ce1"], ["。"]], "en": "He doesn't have a car.", "gap": { "word": "冇", "distractors": ["有", "唔", "未"] } },
      { "words": [["呢度", "ni1 dou6"], ["冇", "mou5"], ["洗手間", "sai2 sau2 gaan1"], ["。"]], "en": "There's no toilet here.", "gap": { "word": "冇", "distractors": ["有", "唔", "係"] } }
    ],
    "mistakes": [
      { "incorrect": "我唔有錢。", "correct": [["我", "ngo5"], ["冇", "mou5"], ["錢", "cin2"], ["。"]], "note": "The negative of 有 is 冇, not 唔有." },
      { "incorrect": "沒有錢。", "correct": [["冇", "mou5"], ["錢", "cin2"], ["。"]], "note": "沒有 is written Chinese." }
    ],
    "related": ["jau5-mou5-question", "m4-negation"],
    "review": { "status": "draft", "reviewers": [] }
  },
  {
    "id": "jau5-mou5-question",
    "pattern": "有冇 + Noun？",
    "jyutping": "jau5 mou5 + Noun?",
    "meaning": "Is there…? / Do you have…?",
    "level": "A1",
    "function": "questions",
    "explanation": "有冇 ('have or not have') turns 有 into a question. Answer 有 for yes or 冇 for no. Ending with 呀 makes it sound friendly.",
    "examples": [
      { "words": [["有冇", "jau5 mou5"], ["奶茶", "naai5 caa4"], ["呀", "aa3"], ["？"]], "en": "Do you have milk tea?", "gap": { "word": "有冇", "distractors": ["係唔係", "幾多", "邊度"] } },
      { "words": [["你", "nei5"], ["有冇", "jau5 mou5"], ["八達通", "baat3 daat6 tung1"], ["呀", "aa3"], ["？"]], "en": "Do you have an Octopus card?", "gap": { "word": "有冇", "distractors": ["係唔係", "幾時", "邊個"] } },
      { "words": [["附近", "fu6 gan6"], ["有冇", "jau5 mou5"], ["銀行", "ngan4 hong4"], ["呀", "aa3"], ["？"]], "en": "Is there a bank nearby?", "gap": { "word": "有冇", "distractors": ["係唔係", "幾多", "點解"] } }
    ],
    "related": ["jau5-mou5", "a-not-a-question"],
    "review": { "status": "draft", "reviewers": [] }
  },
  {
    "id": "a-not-a-question",
    "pattern": "Verb + 唔 + Verb？",
    "jyutping": "Verb + m4 + Verb?",
    "meaning": "yes/no question (A-not-A)",
    "level": "A1",
    "function": "questions",
    "explanation": "The most natural way to ask a yes/no question is to say the verb, then 唔, then the verb again: 去唔去 ('go not go'). For two-syllable words, repeat only the first syllable: 好唔好食. Answer by repeating the verb: 去 or 唔去.",
    "writtenEquivalent": { "cjk": "…嗎？ / 是否", "note": "Written Chinese tends to use 嗎 or 是否." },
    "examples": [
      { "words": [["你", "nei5"], ["去唔去", "heoi3 m4 heoi3"], ["呀", "aa3"], ["？"]], "en": "Are you going?" },
      { "words": [["你", "nei5"], ["係唔係", "hai6 m4 hai6"], ["老師", "lou5 si1"], ["呀", "aa3"], ["？"]], "en": "Are you a teacher?" },
      { "words": [["呢個", "ni1 go3"], ["好唔好食", "hou2 m4 hou2 sik6"], ["呀", "aa3"], ["？"]], "en": "Is this tasty?" }
    ],
    "related": ["jau5-mou5-question", "maa3-question"],
    "review": { "status": "draft", "reviewers": [] }
  },
  {
